-- title:
--   Canonical L-map on a critical graded piece
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota \colon W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to W(k)$; write $B = W(k)/pW(k)$ (the quotient by the ideal generated by $p$) and let $j$ be $\iota$ followed by the reduction $W(k) \to B$. Let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $B$ (a commutative two-dimensional formal group law with an action of $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi^2 = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ [a] = [\sigma a] \circ \varpi$), assumed special for $j$ (its Lie algebra is the direct sum of the $j$- and $j\sigma$-eigenspace submodules, both invertible) and of height $4$ (the kernel of $[p]$ is finite projective of degree $p^4$ at every field-valued point). Assume the two graded pieces of the Cartier module $M = \mathrm{CartierModule}\ p\ \Phi.F$ are complementary, where the $n$-th graded piece consists of those $f$ with $\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}(\tau(c)) \cdot f = \tau(j(\tau(c)))^{p^n}$-homothety applied to $f$ for all $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$, $\tau$ denoting Teichmüller lifts; this complementarity makes $M$ into graded Cartier module data $D$, with Frobenius, the integral Verschiebung $V$, the operator $\Pi$ induced by $\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}$, and the two graded submodules as pieces. Let $L \colon D.M \to D.\mathrm{NMod}$ be an additive map which is a canonical $L$-map: it is a Cartier $L$-map ($L(w \cdot x) = \sigma(w) \cdot L(x)$, $L(Vx) = ((\Pi x, 0))$, and $\lambda \circ L = F$) and it is moreover induced, via base change along a surjection from a $p$-torsion-free ring carrying special graded Cartier module data with a Cartier $L$-map, by that $L$-map. Let $i$ be a natural number which is critical in the sense that $\Pi m \in V M$ for every $m$ in the $i$-th graded piece. Then for every $x$ in the $i$-th graded piece and every $y \in M$ with $Vy = \Pi x$, one has $L(x) = ((y, 0))$, the class of $(y,0)$ in $D.\mathrm{NMod}$.
--
--   This is Lemme 4.2 of Boutot–Carayol's treatment of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: over the characteristic-$p$ base the three defining rules of an $L$-map do not pin $L$ down, and the canonicity condition (descent of an $L$-map from a $p$-torsion-free lift) forces the value $((V^{-1}\Pi x, 0))$ at a critical index. It feeds the construction of the additive map used to identify the $\eta$-piece in degree $0$, and thence the rank-two freeness statements and the determinant computation for rigidified special formal modules.
